ligious myself I I  don't really care too much what houses  of worship are saying presuming that  it's not inciting violence or telling  telling people to go attack others or  commit crimes or whatever the case may  be I don't actually have a problem if  torches wanted to say hey we are going  to take political positions but they  should not be tax-exempt period if  that's the case and there's no way that  that's going to be rolled back any time  soon right I mean the thing is that the  501c3 status is a privilege it's not a  right and they can you know it's a  choice to if you want to say you know  we're going to be a church but we're  going to kind of you know forgo the C 3  status and and pay the taxes you know do  whatever you want you know that the  problem is that this is on the taxpayers  dime and the opposition is trying to  frame this as a free speech issue but  it's really not because when the  taxpayers are footing the bill there are  strings attached you can say whatever  you want on your own dime but not on the  taxpayers dime real quick Sarah what  other ways other than that sex  116 of the appropriations bill is the  Johnson amendment being attacked that's  a great question so there's a few bills  and that are kind of the section 116 as  you mentioned is a writer and a  proponent appropriations bill there's  also the free speech Fairness Act which  has two companion bills in the House and  the Senate and basically it would a  tweak it's not a straight repeal there's  another bill in the house that would be  a straight repeal just completely  striking the language altogether the  free speech Fairness Act weakens it by  allowing nonprofits to get involved in  endorsing politically and opposing  candidates and also spending a de  minimis amount of money on campaigns and  that just opens a whole you know can of  worms of what is de minimis because the  minimis means insubstantial well you  know if you're the university president  of a Notre Dame and you s